WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.220
- So step number one for avoiding

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.970
the collapse of civilization

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:07.031
is: try not to cough on one another.

00:00:07.031 --> 00:00:08.648
(audience laughing)

00:00:08.648 --> 00:00:11.120
Okay, so this is a virus

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.120
and disease epidemics caused
by microbes like this,

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.000
viruses and also bacteria.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.840
These are the things that
precipitated the fall

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.530
of the Roman Empire and of
the Golden Age of Athens

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:27.703
and of most of the empires
of the Native Americans.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.130
And it's sort of surprising

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.100
that when you look at the largest threat

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.750
to the survival of civilizations,
it's something so small.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.170
And in fact, it's so invisibly small

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.670
that viruses and bacteria
weren't understood

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.270
until very recently in history.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.640
And yet, despite their small size,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.900
these have caused more
death and destruction

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.313
than all the famines
and wars put together.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.830
So take as an example, smallpox,

00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:00.530
which was the most destructive
disease in history.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:03.440
It's killed hundreds of millions of people

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.740
between ancient times and 1977

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.700
when it was finally eradicated.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.140
The Romans lost up to a
third of their population

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.300
and parts of their empire,
and about a millennium later,

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.010
what happened is the crusaders came back

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.430
from pillaging distant lands

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.400
and they brought an epidemic
of smallpox to Europe,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.250
Europeans then went over to the New World,

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.810
they brought smallpox to the new world,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.260
and in doing so devastated
the Incas and the Mayans

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.493
and other natives there.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.050
And some of you may know
in 1707, smallpox wiped out

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.503
a third of Iceland.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.540
So similarly the Black
Plague, yersinia pestis,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:44.730
this wiped out a third of
Europe starting in 1347

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.030
and then it kept coming
back to haunt Europe

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.563
century after century.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:54.540
Yellow fever, so badly decimated
Napoleon's armies in Haiti

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.960
that Napoleon gave up the idea

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.790
of having a Western French empire

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.770
just because of yellow fever,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.930
because of this 22,000 crack soldiers

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.363
that he'd sent to Haiti,
21,000 of them died.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:10.300
And so he gave up, and
that's why Napoleon sold

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.430
the Louisiana Territory
to the United States

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:13.967
because he finally, he said,

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:17.427
"I just don't wanna be running this show,

00:02:17.427 --> 00:02:20.220
"confronting diseases that
I no longer understand."

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.900
So he sold it for roughly 5 cents an acre,

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.950
which in a bloodless manner

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.360
doubled the size of the United States.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.760
Okay, and so it goes on and on.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.450
There are these viruses and bacteria

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.660
that have really navigated

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.920
the course of history in major ways.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.180
And when I'm gonna suggest
is that the internet

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.553
is really our key to survival here.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.730
And this is for three reasons,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.790
first, what the internet gives us

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.940
is the ability to work remotely

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.570
and when you can work
from home telepresently

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.410
what this allows you to do
is inhibit viral transmission

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:00.410
by reducing face-to-face
contact, the human-to-human contact.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:02.189
So in the face,

00:03:02.189 --> 00:03:04.010
(audience laughing)

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:05.770
so here's the idea, the next time

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:07.840
that there's a really
killer virus coming our way,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.020
if businesses are prepared in advance,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.250
what they can do is really
leverage telepresence

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.950
to keep supply chains running

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:20.010
with the maximum number of
employees working from home.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.770
Now this isn't gonna keep
everybody off the street,

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:24.730
but it's gonna vastly reduce the density.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.300
And it turns out that when
it comes to epidemics,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.160
that's all you need to do,

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.810
you just need to get things
below a tipping point.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.780
So the reason viruses have
this sort of tipping point

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.120
is because viruses have a limited lifetime

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.240
and a certain probability
of infecting somebody.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.460
And so if you have very low host density,

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.250
then the virus dies before
it can get to a new host.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.780
But as soon as you get
enough people together,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.280
then it can find new hosts

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.730
and you go from some
sort of equilibrium state

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:54.500
into an epidemic, it really blows up.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.520
And in fact, you can see
this sort of thing happening

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.300
every Christmas holiday season

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.750
with people shopping in the malls

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.310
because they all bunched together

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.660
and then you cross over this
population tipping point

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.210
and then everyone gets flu and cold.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.690
Okay, so now here's the problem.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:12.270
In the past, societies
have reacted to epidemics

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.420
by bunching together.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.330
So, for example, in medieval Europe,

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.830
when the Black Plague hit
and other plagues like it,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.070
warring religious factions

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.110
who spent all their
time killing each other,

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:30.550
would show solidarity in
the face of all this death

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:32.440
by marching together
in the streets together

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.150
to show that the Catholics
and the Protestants

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.530
could be friends in
the face of the plague.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:39.913
Well, that was a real
misstep in terms of density.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:43.160
And it turns out that the Native Americans

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:47.040
in a show of goodwill, they
would gather in the tents

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.960
of people who were infected with smallpox.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.640
So everyone would gather
together and again,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.753
unfortunately that was a gesture
that was sort of ill-fated.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:58.820
And so this is exactly the fear

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.680
that all major medical centers have.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.430
The next time we have a
major new strain hitting us,

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.560
whether it's avian flu or
swine flu or something,

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.230
the big fear that medical centers have

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:10.920
is that everybody with a cough

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.710
is gonna come flocking into the med center

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.220
to get checked out, and
this is really dangerous.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.400
And so I think this is the
second great opportunity

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.620
afforded to us by the
net besides telepresence

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.280
is telemedicine whereby with increasingly

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.810
sophisticated technologies,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.950
we don't have to have patients coming in

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:29.960
and bunching up together,

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.163
but instead we can have
diagnosis from a distance.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.610
Okay, so the telepresence
and the telemedicine

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:40.080
are very useful because they
keep the population density

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.890
below a tipping point.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.180
I think there's a third benefit

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.440
that we get from the internet,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.370
which is we can optimally direct resources

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.270
when there is an outbreak.

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.560
So you may know that the
Center for Disease Control

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:56.940
tracks the flu by tracking

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.310
what happens at the local hospitals.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:02.250
Now the thing is, it takes
two weeks for the CDC

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.460
to put together their report.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.690
It lags the actual flu
outbreak by two weeks.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.760
So Google came up with a better idea.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.690
And what they do is
they track where people

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:13.290
are searching for terms
related to the flu.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.790
So if they're searching
for information on symptoms

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:17.050
or medicines or something,

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:19.450
it turns out that over
the course of the nation,

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:21.210
that serves as an excellent proxy

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.560
for where there's a flu outbreak.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.220
So while the CDC's
report lags by two weeks,

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.410
Google's lags by only a day.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:30.290
So this gives us a very rapid way

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:33.280
to know dynamically
exactly where the flu is

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.563
and where the outbreaks are happening.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:40.420
Okay, so unlike previous generations

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.730
that were brought down by disease,

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:44.540
especially because they
didn't know how to react

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.400
in terms of density and sparseness,

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.290
we can now do better
because of the internet.

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:53.220
If we're well prepared when
the next epidemic arrives,

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.630
we can fluidly shift
into a self quarantined

00:06:56.630 --> 00:07:00.480
telepresence society in
which the microbes fail

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.180
by dint of host sparseness.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.460
And so there's a lot of talk of course

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:07.050
about the ills of social isolation

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.520
and everybody sitting on Facebook.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:09.860
But whatever those ills are,

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:13.090
it bodes a lot worse for the
microbes than it does for us.

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:15.840
So although we're well into this step,

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.330
there's work to be done if we
want to save our civilization,

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:21.910
businesses really need to work

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:24.970
on developing their disaster plans

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:28.000
and their work from home epidemic plans.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.900
I wrote a paper on this
in the journal Nature

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:30.840
about five years ago

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.820
and I've been watching as
businesses have been doing this,

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:34.690
I've been monitoring sort
of how this is going.

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:37.170
Some businesses are doing
it, most aren't still.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.540
It's really important to try
to get businesses to do this,

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.870
and it's extremely easy to test, right?

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.580
To work out the kinks by having
everybody work from home.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.310
And then the second thing as a society,

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:48.830
we really need to keep
developing telemedicine

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:50.757
and similar ideas like that.

